\subsection{5.1, Eigenvalues and eigenvectors}

<exercises>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="no">
      <p>If <m>A</m> is an <m>n \times n</m> matrix and <m>\det(A)=2</m>, then <m>2</m> is an
      eigenvalue of <m>A</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>False.  For example, <m>A=\begin{pmatrix}4 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1/2\end{pmatrix}</m> has <m>\det(A)=2</m> but its eigenvalues
        are <m>4</m> and <m>\dfrac{1}{2}</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="yes">
      <p>Suppose <m>A</m> is a <m>n \times n</m> matrix and <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> are nonzero
      vectors in <m>{\bf R}^{n}</m> satisfying <m>Au = 2u</m> and <m>Av = 3v</m>.  Then <m>\{u, v\}</m> must
      be linearly independent.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>True: we have implicitly been told that <m>2</m> and <m>3</m> are eigenvalues of <m>A</m>,
        where <m>u</m> is a <m>2</m>-eigenvector and <m>v</m> is a <m>3</m>-eigenvector.  Since eigenvectors
        corresponding to different eigenvalues are automatically linearly independent,
        the set <m>\{u,v\}</m> must be linearly independent.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="yes">
      <p>The matrices <m>A=\begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp -1\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>B=\begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 20 \\ 0 \amp -1\end{pmatrix}</m> have
      the same eigenvalues.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>True.  Each matrix has eigenvalues <m>\lambda=2</m> and <m>\lambda = -1</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="no">
      <p>If <m>A</m> is an <m>n \times n</m> matrix and <m>v</m> and <m>w</m> are eigenvectors
      of <m>A</m>, then <m>v+w</m> is also an eigenvector of <m>A</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>False.  For example, if <m>A= \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}</m> then <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m>
        are eigenvectors, but <m>A \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m> so <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m> is not an
        eigenvector.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="no">
      <p>The zero vector is an eigenvector for every square matrix.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>False.  By definition of eigenvector, the zero vector is never an eigenvector
        for any matrix.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Which of the following vectors (if any) are eigenvectors for
      <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}35 \amp -32 \\ 40 \amp -37\end{pmatrix}</m>?</p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p><m>v_1 = \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p><m>v_2 = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ -1\end{pmatrix}</m></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p><m>v_3 = \begin{pmatrix}4 \\ 5\end{pmatrix}</m></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We check each vector one-by-one.
        <md><mrow>Av_1 = \begin{pmatrix}35 \amp -32 \\ 40 \amp -37\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}38 \\ 43\end{pmatrix},
          \quad \textup{which is not a scalar multiple of } v_1</mrow></md>.
        <md><mrow>Av_2 = \begin{pmatrix}35 \amp -32 \\ 40 \amp -37\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ -1\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}-3 \\ -3\end{pmatrix} = 3v_2</mrow></md>.
        <md><mrow>Av_3 = \begin{pmatrix}35 \amp -32 \\ 40 \amp -37\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}4 \\ 5\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}-20 \\ -25\end{pmatrix} = -5v_3</mrow></md>.
        From the
        computations, we conclude that <m>v_2</m> and <m>v_3</m> are eigenvectors of <m>A</m>, but <m>v_1</m>
        is not an eigenvector of <m>A</m> because <m>Av_1</m> is not a scalar multiple of <m>v_1</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the <m>2</m>-eigenspace for the matrix
        <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}0 \amp 13 \amp 12 \\ 1/4 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1/2 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We need to find <m>\Nul(A-2I)</m>.  For this, we note
        <md><mrow>A - 2I = \begin{pmatrix}0 \amp 13 \amp 12 \\ 1/4 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1/2 \amp 0\end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}
        = \begin{pmatrix}-2 \amp 13 \amp 12 \\ 1/4 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1/2 \amp -2\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        Now:
        <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{r|r}A-2I \amp 0\end{array}\right) =
          \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}-2 \amp 13 \amp 12 \amp 0 \\ 1/4 \amp -2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1/2 \amp -2 \amp 0\end{array}\right)
          \xrightarrow[]{RREF}
          \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}1 \amp 0 \amp -32 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -4 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)</mrow></md>.
        This gives us <m>x_1 - 32x_3 = 0</m> and <m>x_2 - 4 x_3 = 0</m> with <m>x_3</m> free.  The parametric
        vector form is
        <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}32x_3 \\ 4x_3 \\ x_3\end{pmatrix} = x_3 \begin{pmatrix}32 \\ 4 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        Therefore, the <m>2</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m> is
        <md><mrow>\Nul(A-2I) = \Span \left\{\begin{pmatrix}32 \\ 4 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}\right\}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>How many different eigenvalues can an <m>n \times n</m> matrix have?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The maximum is <m>n</m>.  Since eigenvectors for different eigenvalues are
          automatically linearly independent, and since no set of vectors in
          <m>{\bf R}^{n}</m> can be linearly independent if it has more than <m>n</m> vectors in
          it, we cannot have more than <m>n</m> different eigenvalues.  It is possible to
          have exactly <m>n</m> different eigenvalues, for example the <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix
          below has <m>3</m> different eigenvalues.
        <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 3\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Consider the matrix <m>A</m> for reflection of <m>{\bf R}^2</m> about the line <m>y = 7x</m>.
      What are its eigenvalues?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>The eigenvalues are <m>\lambda = 1</m> and <m>\lambda = -1</m>.  The matrix does not
        change any vector along the line <m>y = 7x</m>.  In other words, <m>Av = v</m> for every
        vector <m>v</m> along the line <m>y = 7x</m>. Therefore, <m>\lambda = 1</m> is an eigenvalue.</p>
      <p>The matrix flips every vector on the line through the origin that is
        perpendicular to the line <m>y = 7x</m>.  In other words, <m>Av = -v</m> for every
        vector <m>v</m> along the line <m>y = -x/7</m>.  Therefore, <m>\lambda = -1</m> is an
        eigenvalue.</p>
      <p>Since a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix can have at most <m>2</m> eigenvalues, we conclude that
        <m>1</m> and <m>-1</m> are the only eigenvalues of <m>A</m>.</p>
      <p>Note that the eigenvalues and reasoning would be identical if we changed the
        line <m>y = 7x</m> to a different line <m>L</m> through the origin.  The eigenvalues
        would still be <m>1</m> and <m>-1</m>, the <m>1</m>-eigenspace would be <m>L</m>, and the
        <m>(-1)</m>-eigenspace would be the line through the origin perpendicular to <m>L</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Without doing any major computations, write one real eigenvalue for
      each of the following matrices.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p><m>A = \begin{pmatrix}6 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ -4 \amp 6 \amp 0 \\ 6 \amp 6 \amp 6\end{pmatrix}</m></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Since <m>A</m> is lower-triangular, its eigenvalues are its
            diagonal entries.  Each of these is <m>6</m>, so the only eigenvalue of
            <m>A</m> is <m>\lambda = 6</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The
            standard matrix <m>A</m> for the linear transformation <m>T</m> that satisfies
          <md><mrow>T(e_1) = e_3, \quad T(e_2) = 2e_2 - e_3, \quad T(e_3) = -e_2
              +2e_3</mrow></md>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We see <m>e_2+e_3</m> is an eigenvector corresponding to
              eigenvalue <m>\lambda = 1</m> since
          <md><mrow>T(e_2+e_3) = 2e_2-e_3 + (-e_2 + 2e_3) = e_2 + e_3</mrow></md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p><m>A = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 4 \amp 5 \amp 6 \\ 1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 4 \amp 5 \amp 6 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}.</m></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>The first two rows of the square matrix <m>A</m> are the
            same, so without doing any work we know that <m>A</m> is not invertible,
            therefore <m>\lambda = 0</m> is an eigenvalue of <m>A</m> since the equation
            <m>Ax = 0</m> has a non-trivial solution.</p>
        <p>Alternatively, we could have noted from the form of <m>A</m> that
            <m>A(e_1-e_2) = 0</m>, which shows that <m>\lambda = 0</m> is an eigenvalue
            and <m>e_1 - e_2</m> is a corresponding eigenvector.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Let <m>T: {\bf R}^2 \to {\bf R}^2</m> be the linear transformation
          which reflects across the line <m>L</m> drawn below, and let <m>A</m> be the
          standard matrix for <m>T</m>.  Answer both questions below without
          doing any computations.</p>
      <image source="images/q6_18s-line.pdf">
        <shortdescription><!-- FILL: concise image description --></shortdescription>
        <description><p><!-- FILL: complete image description --></p></description>
      </image>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Find all eigenvalues of <m>A</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>The equation of the line was not given, and it is
                irrelevant.  The eigenvalues of <m>A</m> are <m>\lambda_1 = 1</m> and
                <m>\lambda_2 = -1</m>, since <m>A</m> fixes every vector along the line
                <m>L</m> while <m>A</m> flips every vector perpendicular to <m>L</m>.</p>
        <p>In the graph, <m>v_1</m> corresponds to <m>\lambda_1 = 1</m>, while
                <m>v_2</m> corresponds to <m>\lambda_2= -1</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>For each eigenvalue of <m>A</m>, draw one eigenvector on
              the graph above.  Your eigenvector does not need to be perfect,
              but it should be reasonably accurate.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>We graph eigenvectors below.</p>
        <image source="images/q6_18s-answer">
          <shortdescription><!-- FILL: concise image description --></shortdescription>
          <description><p><!-- FILL: complete image description --></p></description>
        </image>
        <p>Many answers are possible: <m>v_1</m> can be any nonzero vector on
                <m>L</m> (going up-to-right or down-to-left), while <m>v_2</m> can be
                any nonzero vector perpendicular to <m>L</m> (going up-to-left or
                down-to-right).</p>
        <p>Note: Algebraically, the
                problem would have been much more painful!
        The line
                is actually the line <m>y = \dfrac{3}{7} x</m>, the matrix is
                <m>A= \dfrac{1}{29} \begin{pmatrix}20 \amp 21 \\ 21 \amp -20\end{pmatrix}</m>, and the eigenvectors
                drawn are <m>v_1 = \begin{pmatrix}7/3 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>v_2 = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 7/3\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose <m>A</m> is a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix, and that <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> are vectors
      in the <m>(-2)</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m> and the <m>5</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m>, respectively.
      Find <m>A(8u - 4v)</m> in terms of <m>u</m> and <m>v</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We are given that <m>Au = -2u</m> and <m>Av = 5v</m>, so we use linearity:
        <md><mrow>A(8u-4v) = A(8u) - A(4v) = 8Au - 4Av = -16u -20v</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        There is one <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix <m>A</m> with the property that
        <md><mrow>\Nul(A-2I) = \Span \left\{\begin{pmatrix}7 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}\right\},
          \quad \textup{ and } \quad
          \Nul(A+I) = \Span \left\{\begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 3\end{pmatrix} \right\}</mrow></md>.
        Find <m>A</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We are told the <m>2</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m> is spanned by <m>\begin{pmatrix}7 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m> and that the
          <m>(-1)</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m> is spanned by <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ -3\end{pmatrix}</m>, so
        <md><mrow>Au = 2u = \begin{pmatrix}14 \\ 2\end{pmatrix} \quad \textup{ and } \quad
          Av = -v = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ -3\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        Recall from section 3.3 that if <m>M</m> is any <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix, then its first
          column is <m>Me_1</m> and its second column is <m>Me_2</m> (this can also
          be seen directly from the definition of <q>matrix times vector</q> multiplication), so
          we need to find <m>A\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>A \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For this, we write the standard basis vectors as linear combinations of the
          eigenvectors <m>u = \begin{pmatrix}7 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>v = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ -3\end{pmatrix}</m>.
        <md><mrow>\textup{For } \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}: \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}7 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp -3 \amp 0\end{array}\right)
            \xrightarrow[]{RREF} \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp 0 \amp 3/22 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1/22\end{array}\right),
            \quad \textup{ so } \quad \dfrac{3}{22}u + \dfrac{1}{22} v
            = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>,
        <md>
          <mrow>A \begin{pmatrix}1</mrow>
          <mrow>0\end{pmatrix}
            \amp  = A \left(\dfrac{3}{22}u + \dfrac{1}{22} v\right)
              = \dfrac{3}{22} Au + \dfrac{1}{22} Av
              = \dfrac{3}{22} \begin{pmatrix}14</mrow>
          <mrow>2\end{pmatrix} + \dfrac{1}{22} \begin{pmatrix}-1</mrow>
          <mrow>3\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp   = \begin{pmatrix}41/22</mrow>
          <mrow>9/22\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <md><mrow>\textup{For } \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}: \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}7 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp -3 \amp 1\end{array}\right)
            \xrightarrow[]{RREF} \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp 0 \amp 1/22 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -7/22\end{array}\right),
            \quad \textup{ so } \quad \dfrac{1}{22}u - \dfrac{7}{22} v
            = \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>,
        <md>
          <mrow>A \begin{pmatrix}0</mrow>
          <mrow>1\end{pmatrix}
            \amp  = A \left(\dfrac{1}{22}u - \dfrac{7}{22} v\right)
              = \dfrac{1}{22} Au - \dfrac{7}{22} Av
              = \dfrac{1}{22} \begin{pmatrix}14</mrow>
          <mrow>2\end{pmatrix} - \dfrac{7}{22} \begin{pmatrix}-1</mrow>
          <mrow>3\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp   = \begin{pmatrix}21/22</mrow>
          <mrow>-19/22\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Therefore,
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}A\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp \\ \amp 0\end{pmatrix} \amp A\begin{pmatrix}0 \amp \\ \amp 1\end{pmatrix}\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}41/22 \amp 21/22 \\ 9/22 \amp -19/22\end{pmatrix}
          = \dfrac{1}{22} \begin{pmatrix}41 \amp 21 \\ 9 \amp -19\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
      <p>This problem's matrix <m>A</m> is an example of a diagonalizable matrix.  We will
      find a different way to compute <m>A</m> in section 5.4.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</exercises>\setcounter{problem}{0}

\newpage

\subsection{5.2, The characteristic polynomial}
<exercises>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="no">
      <p>Every <m>n \times n</m> matrix has a real eigenvalue.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>False.  For example, the <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix for rotation by <m>\pi/2</m> radians
        counterclockwise has no real eigenvalues.  The matrix is <m>\begin{pmatrix}0 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}</m>,
        and we can compute that its characteristic equation is
        <m>0 = \det(A-\lambda I) = \lambda^2+1</m>, so its eigenvalues are <m>\pm i</m>.  We
        discuss complex eigenvalues in section 5.5.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="no">
      <p>Every <m>n \times n</m> matrix has <m>n</m> distinct eigenvalues.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>False.  The <m>2 \times 2</m> identity matrix <m>I = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</m> has only one
      eigenvalue, namely <m>\lambda = 1</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="yes">
      <p>If <m>\lambda</m> is an eigenvalue of a matrix <m>A</m>, then the nullity
      of <m>A- \lambda I</m> is the dimension of the <m>\lambda</m>-eigenspace.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>True.  By definition, the <m>\lambda</m>-eigenspace is the null space of
      <m>A-\lambda I</m>, so the dimension of the <m>\lambda</m>-eigenspace
      is precisely the dimension of the null space of <m>A-\lambda I</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>What are the eigenvalues for the standard matrix for a reflection?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>They are always <m>1</m> and <m>-1</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>What are the eigenvalues and eigenvectors for the <m>n \times n</m> zero matrix?</p>
      <p>\ifanswers \newpage \fi</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>Let <m>A</m> be the <m>n \times n</m> zero matrix.  Then <m>A-\lambda I</m> is the
        <m>n \times n</m> diagonal matrix whose diagonal entries are all <m>-\lambda</m>, so its characteristic
        polynomial is <m>(-\lambda)^n</m>.  Therefore, the only eigenvalue is <m>\lambda = 0</m>.  The
        <m>0</m>-eigenspace is all of <m>{\bf R}^{n}</m>, since <m>Ax = 0</m> for every <m>x</m> in <m>{\bf R}^{n}</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Find the eigenvalues of
      <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp -5 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 8 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}.</m></p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We solve for <m>0 = \det(A-\lambda I)</m> using the cofactor expansion
        along the second row.
        <md>
          <mrow>0 \amp  = \det \begin{pmatrix}1-\lambda \amp \ \amp 2 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp -5-\lambda \amp \ \amp 0</mrow>
          <mrow>1 \amp 8 \amp -\lambda\end{pmatrix}
        = (-1)^{4}(-5-\lambda) \det\begin{pmatrix}1-\lambda \amp \ \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>1 \amp -\lambda\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp
        = (-5-\lambda)\Big(\lambda^2-\lambda-1\Big)</mrow>
        </md>.
        Thus, <m>\lambda = -5</m> is an eigenvalue, and so are the solutions to <m>\lambda^2-\lambda -1 = 0</m>.  For this, we use the quadratic formula to get
        <md><mrow>\lambda = \frac{-(-1) \pm \sqrt{(-1)^2-4(-1)(1)}}{2}
        = \frac{1 \pm \sqrt{5}}{2}</mrow></md>.
        The eiegenvalues are <m>\lambda = -5</m>,
        <m>\lambda = \dfrac{1 + \sqrt{5}}{2}</m>, and
        <m>\lambda = \dfrac{1 - \sqrt{5}}{2}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Find the eigenvalues of <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}9 \amp 9 \\ 9 \amp 9\end{pmatrix}</m>.  For each eigenvalue, find
      a basis for the corresponding eigenspace.</p>
      <p>\ifanswers \newpage \fi</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The characteristic polynomial can be computed by doing the full computation
          of <m>\det(A- \lambda I)</m> or using the shortcut <m>2 \times 2</m> formula.  It is
        <md><mrow>\det(A-\lambda I) = \lambda^2 - \textup{Tr}(A) \lambda + \det(A)
            = \lambda^2 - 18 \lambda + 0 = \lambda(\lambda-18)</mrow></md>.
        Therefore,
          the eigenvalues are <m>\lambda = 0</m> and <m>\lambda = 18</m>.  We row-reduce
        <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rr|r}A-0I \amp \ \amp 0\end{array}\right)= \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}9 \amp 9 \amp 0 \\ 9 \amp 9 \amp 0\end{array}\right) \to \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)</mrow></md>,
        so <m>\left\{\begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m> is a basis for the <m>0</m>-eigenspace.
          Also,
        <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rr|r}A-18I \amp \ \amp 0\end{array}\right)= \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}-9 \amp 9 \amp 0 \\ 9 \amp -9 \amp 0\end{array}\right) \to \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)</mrow></md>,
        so <m>\left\{\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m> is a basis for the <m>18</m>-eigenspace.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Consider the matrix <m>A</m> for rotation by <m>\pi/2</m> radians about the <m>z</m>-axis in
      <m>{\bf R}^3</m>. What are its real eigenvalues?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We can think of <m>A</m> as the matrix that rotates the <q>xy-piece</q> of the vector
          by <m>\pi/2</m> radians, while keeping the <m>z</m>-coordinate fixed.  This means
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        Its characteristic polynomial is the following, which we compute using the cofactor
          expansion along the third row.
        <md>
          <mrow>\det(A - \lambda I)
            \amp  = \det\begin{pmatrix}-\lambda \amp \ \amp -1 \amp 0</mrow>
          <mrow>1 \amp -\lambda \amp \ \amp 0</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0 \amp 1-\lambda\end{pmatrix} = (-1)^{3+3}(1-\lambda)\Big[(-\lambda)(-\lambda)-1(-1)\Big]</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = (1-\lambda)(\lambda^2 + 1)</mrow>
        </md>.
        The characteristic polynomial factors as <m>(1-\lambda)(\lambda+i)(\lambda-i)</m>,
          so the only real eigenvalue is <m>\lambda = 1</m>.  Complex eigenvalues will be
          covered in a later section of the textbook.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>In each case, find the eigenvalues of the matrix <m>A</m> and a basis
      for each eigenspace.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p><m>A</m> the <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix that reflects vectors across
          the line <m>y = x</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>The eigenvalues of <m>A</m> are <m>\lambda = 1</m> and
            <m>\lambda = -1</m>, since <m>A</m> fixes vectors along the line <m>y=x</m> and
            flips vectors perpendicular to the line <m>y=x</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p><m>A</m> the <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix that rotates vectors by
          <m>180^\circ</m> around the <m>z</m>-axis, where we label points in
          <m>{\bf R}^3</m> as <m>(x, y, z)</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>The eigenvalues of <m>A</m> are <m>\lambda = 1</m> and
            <m>\lambda = -1</m>, since <m>A</m> fixes vectors along the <m>z</m>-axis while
            <m>Ae_1 = -e_1</m> and <m>Ae_2 = -e_2</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>A</m> be the <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix satisfying
        <md><mrow>A\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 3\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}-2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}, \qquad A \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        What are the eigenvalues of <m>A</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We find <m>A</m> first.  Since <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 3\end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 3\end{pmatrix}</m>,
          linearity gives us
        <md><mrow>A\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} = A \left(\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 3\end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 3\end{pmatrix}\right) = A \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 3\end{pmatrix} -
            3 A \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}-2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} - 3 \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 2\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}-5 \\ -5\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        Therefore,
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}A\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp \\ \amp 0\end{pmatrix} \amp A \amp \begin{pmatrix}0 \amp \\ \amp 1\end{pmatrix}\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}-5 \amp 1 \\ -5 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        For the eigenvalues of <m>A</m>, we solve:
        <md>
          <mrow>0 \amp  = \det(A-\lambda I) = \lambda^2- \Tr(A)\lambda + \det(A)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = \lambda^2 + 3 \lambda -5,</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  \lambda = \dfrac{-3 \pm \sqrt{9-4(-5)(1)}}{2}
                 = \dfrac{-3 \pm \sqrt{29}}{2}</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Find the eigenvalues of the matrix <m>A</m> that first reflects
      vectors in <m>{\bf R}^2</m> across the line <m>y = -x</m>, then rotates
      <m>\pi/2</m> radians counterclockwise, then scales the first coordinate
      by a factor of <m>4</m> while keeping the second coordinate fixed.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        One way to do this problem is to compute <m>A</m>.
        <md>
          <mrow>A \amp  = \begin{pmatrix}4 \amp 0</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}0 \amp -1</mrow>
          <mrow>1 \amp 0\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}0 \amp -1</mrow>
          <mrow>-1 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}
                = \begin{pmatrix}0 \amp -4</mrow>
          <mrow>1 \amp 0\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}0 \amp -1</mrow>
          <mrow>-1 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}
                = \begin{pmatrix}4 \amp 0</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp -1\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
        </md>.
        Therefore, the eigenvalues of <m>A</m> are <m>\lambda = 4</m> and
          <m>\lambda = -1</m>.
      </p>
      <p>Alternatively, we could have
        computed <m>Ae_1</m> and <m>Ae_2</m> geometrically and found that
        <m>Ae_1 = 4 e_1</m> and <m>Ae_2 = -e_2</m>, so <m>e_1</m> and <m>e_2</m> are
        eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalues <m>4</m> and <m>-1</m>
        respectively.  Since a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix has at most <m>2</m> distinct
        eigenvalues, these are the only eigenvalues of <m>A</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are <m>n \times n</m> matrices with the same
      characteristic polynomial.  Do <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> have the same eigenvalues?
      Answer sometimes, always, or never, and justify your answer.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>Always.  The eigenvalues of a square matrix are the roots of its
        characteristic polynomial, so if <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> have the same characteristic
        polynomial then their eigenvalues will be the same.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</exercises>\setcounter{problem}{0}

\newpage

\subsection{5.4, Diagonalization}

<exercises>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="yes">
      <p>If <m>A</m> is a <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix with eigenvalues <m>0</m>, <m>1</m>, and <m>2</m>, then
      <m>A</m> is diagonalizable.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>True: any <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix with <m>3</m> real eigenvalues is automatically
        diagonalizable.  One way to see this is that each of the <m>3</m> eigenvalues must
        have a nonzero eigenvector in their eigenspace, and eigenvectors corresponding
        to different eigenvalues are linearly independent, therefore we obtain <m>3</m>
        linearly independent eigenvectors.  In other words, we obtain a basis for
        <m>{\bf R}^{3}</m> consisting of eigenvectors of <m>A</m>, which is equivalent to the
        property that <m>A</m> is diagonalizable.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="no">
      <p>If <m>\lambda</m> is an eigenvalue of a matrix <m>A</m>, it is possible
      that the <m>\lambda</m>-eigenspace is <m>0</m>-dimensional.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>False.  It is a fundamental fact that the geometric multiplicity of any
        eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m> is at least <m>1</m>.  By definition of eigenvalue, the
        equation <m>Ax = \lambda x</m> must have a non-trivial solution, therefore
        <m>(A-\lambda I)x = 0</m> has a non-trivial solution, which means that
        <m>\Nul(A-\lambda I)</m> is at least <m>1</m>-dimensional.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="no">
      <p>All diagonalizable matrices are invertible.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>False: for example, zero matrix is diagonalizable (in fact, diagonal!) but is
        not invertible.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="no">
      <p>All invertible matrices are diagonalizable.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>False: for example, take <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</m>. Note <m>\det(A) = 1</m>, so <m>A</m> is
        invertible.  However, the only eigenvalue of <m>A</m> is <m>\lambda = 1</m>, and we can
        compute that the <m>1</m>-eigenspace is only <m>1</m>-dimensional, so <m>A</m> is not
        diagonalizable.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="yes">
      <p>All diagonal <m>n \times n</m> matrices are diagonalizable.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>True: if <m>A</m> is diagonal, then <m>A = I A I^{-1}</m>, so <m>A</m> is diagonalizable.  In
        other words, <m>A = CDC^{-1}</m> where <m>C</m> is the <m>n \times n</m> identity matrix and
        <m>D</m> is <m>A</m> itself!</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="no">
      <p>All upper-triangular <m>n \times n</m> matrices are diagonalizable.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        False: for example,
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        The only eigenvalue of <m>A</m> is <m>\lambda = 2</m>, but we can
        compute that the <m>2</m>-eigenspace is only <m>1</m>-dimensional, so <m>A</m> is not
        diagonalizable.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Find <m>\begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 6 \\ 0 \amp -1\end{pmatrix}^{100}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <m>A</m> is <m>2 \times 2</m> with <m>2</m> different eigenvalues <m>\lambda = 2</m> and
          <m>\lambda = -1</m>, so <m>A</m> is diagonalizable.
        <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{r|r}A-2I \amp 0\end{array}\right) = \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}0 \amp 6 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -3 \amp 0\end{array}\right) \xrightarrow[]{RREF}
            \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right), \quad 2\textup{-eigenspace}
            = \Span \left\{\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} \right\}</mrow></md>.
        <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{r|r}A+I \amp 0\end{array}\right) = \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}3 \amp 6 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right) \xrightarrow[]{RREF}
            \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right), \quad (-1)\textup{-eigenspace}
            = \Span \left\{\begin{pmatrix}-2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} \right\}</mrow></md>.
        Therefore, <m>A = CDC^{-1}</m> where <m>C = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp -2 \\ 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</m> and
          <m>D = \begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1\end{pmatrix}</m>.  Using the repeated cancellations of <m>C</m> with
          <m>C^{-1}</m> when expanding <m>A^{100}</m>, we get
        <md>
          <mrow>A^{100} \amp  = (C D C^{-1})^{100} = C D^{100} C^{-1}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = C \begin{pmatrix}2^{100} \amp 0</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp (-1)^{100}\end{pmatrix} C^{-1}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp -2</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}2^{100} \amp 0</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 2</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp -2</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}2^{100} \amp 2^{101}</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = \begin{pmatrix}2^{100} \amp 2^{101}-2</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Let <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \amp 0 \amp -2 \\ 0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 3 \amp 0 \amp 4\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Find the eigenvalues of <m>A</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We solve <m>0=\det(A-\lambda I)</m>.
          <md>
            <mrow>0 \amp  = \det\begin{pmatrix}-1-\lambda \amp 0 \amp -2</mrow>
            <mrow>0 \amp 2-\lambda \amp 0</mrow>
            <mrow>3 \amp 0 \amp 4-\lambda\end{pmatrix} =
                    (2-\lambda)(-1)^{4} \det\begin{pmatrix}-1-\lambda \amp -2</mrow>
            <mrow>3 \amp 4-\lambda\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp  =(2-\lambda)\left((-1-\lambda)(4-\lambda) + 6\right)
                     = (2-\lambda)(\lambda^2-3\lambda -4+6)</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp  = (2-\lambda)(\lambda^2-3\lambda+2)
                     = (2-\lambda)(\lambda-2)(\lambda-1)</mrow>
          </md>
          So <m>\lambda = 1</m> and <m>\lambda = 2</m> are the eigenvalues.
        </p>
        <p>\underline{<m>\lambda = 1</m>}:
            <m>\left(\begin{array}{r|r}A-I \amp 0\end{array}\right) = \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}-2 \amp 0 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 3 \amp 0 \amp 3 \amp 0\end{array}\right)
            \xrightarrow[\textup{then } R_1 = -R_1
            /2]{R_3=R_3+\frac{3}{2}R_1}\left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)</m> with
            solution <m>x_1= -x_3</m>, <m>x_2=0</m>, <m>x_3=x_3</m>.  The <m>1</m>-eigenspace has
            basis <m>\left\{ \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} \right\}</m>.</p>
        <p>
          \underline{<m>\lambda = 2</m>}:
          <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{r|r}A-2I \amp 0\end{array}\right) = \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}-3 \amp 0 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 3 \amp 0 \amp 2 \amp 0\end{array}\right)
                  \xrightarrow[\textup{then } R_1= -R_1/3]{R_3=R_3+R_1} \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{2}{3} \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)</mrow></md>
          with solution
                <m>x_1= -\frac{2}{3}x_3</m>, \ <m>x_2=x_2</m>, \ <m>x_3=x_3</m>.  The
                <m>2</m>-eigenspace has basis
                <m>\left\{ \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix}-2/3 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Find a basis for each eigenspace of <m>A</m>.  Mark your
          answers clearly.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p><m>A</m> is diagonalizable; <m>A=PDP^{-1}</m> where
              <m>P = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \amp 0 \amp -2/3 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</m> and
              <m>D= \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>\enspace Is <m>A</m> diagonalizable?  If your answer is yes,
          find a diagonal matrix <m>D</m> and an invertible matrix <m>P</m> so that
          <m>A = PDP^{-1}</m>.  If your answer is no, justify why <m>A</m> is not
          diagonalizable.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Let <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 4 \amp 6 \\ 0 \amp 2 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 4\end{pmatrix}</m>.  Is <m>A</m> diagonalizable?
      If so, find a basis of <m>{\bf R}^3</m> consisting of eigenvectors of <m>A</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
      <ol marker="(a)">
        <li>
          <p>The matrix is upper-triangular, so the eigenvalues are
            the diagonal entries: <m>\lambda_1 = -1</m>, <m>\lambda_2 = 4</m>, and
            <m>\lambda_3 = 5</m>.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            We find a basis for each eigenspace.
            <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{r|r}A-\lambda_1I \amp 0\end{array}\right) =
                \left(\begin{array}{r|r}A+I \amp 0\end{array}\right) =
                \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}0 \amp 5 \amp 10 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 5 \amp 10 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 6 \amp 0\end{array}\right)
                \xrightarrow[]{RREF}
                \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)</mrow></md>.
            This means <m>x_2 = x_3 = 0</m>, so a basis for <m>(-1)</m>-eigenspace
              is <m>\left\{\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>.
            <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{r|r}A-\lambda_2I \amp 0\end{array}\right) =
                \left(\begin{array}{r|r}A-4I \amp 0\end{array}\right) =
                \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}-5 \amp 5 \amp 10 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 10 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0\end{array}\right)
                \xrightarrow[]{RREF}
                \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)</mrow></md>.
            So <m>x_1 = x_2</m> (with <m>x_2</m> free) and <m>x_3 = 0</m>, so
              a basis for the <m>4</m>-eigenspace is <m>\left\{\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{r|r}A-\lambda_3I \amp 0\end{array}\right) =
                \left(\begin{array}{r|r}A-5I \amp 0\end{array}\right) = \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}-6 \amp 5 \amp 10 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \amp 10 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)
                \xrightarrow[]{RREF} \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}1 \amp 0 \amp -10 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -10 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)</mrow></md>.
            Thus
              <m>x_1 = 10x_3</m> and <m>x_2 = 10x_3</m> (<m>x_3</m> free). Basis for
              <m>5</m>-eigenspace is <m>\left\{ \begin{pmatrix}10 \\ 10 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} \right\}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Yes.  We form <m>C</m> using the three eigenvectors we found
              in part (b), and <m>D</m> is the diagonal matrix with corresponding
              eigenvalues.
            <md><mrow>C = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 1 \amp 10 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 10 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix} \qquad
                D = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 4 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 5\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Write a <m>4 \times 4</m> matrix <m>A</m> with only one real eigenvalue <m>\lambda = 2</m>, so
      that the <m>2</m>-eigenspace for <m>A</m> is a three-dimensional subspace of <m>{\bf
        R}^4</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We need <m>\lambda=2</m> to be the only eigenvalue, and we need <m>\left(\begin{array}{r|r}A-2I \amp 0\end{array}\right)</m> to
          have three free variables and thus one pivot.  For example,
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Write a <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix <m>B</m> with the property that <m>e_1</m>, <m>e_2</m>, and <m>e_3</m>
      are eigenvectors of <m>B</m>.  Must <m>B</m> be diagonalizable?  Justify your answer.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Endless possibilities.  For example,
        <md><mrow>B = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}, \qquad B = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 5 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp -3\end{pmatrix}, \qquad
          B = \begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 3 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 3\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        Note that <m>B</m> must be diagonalizable because
        it is a <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix with <m>3</m> linearly independent eigenvectors, namely
        <m>e_1</m>, <m>e_2</m>, <m>e_3</m>.  In truth, <m>B</m> satisfies the conditions of this problem if
        and only if it is a diagonal <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Find the <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix <m>A</m> whose eigenspaces are drawn below.
      Fully simplify your answer.</p>
      <p>(to be clear: the dashed line is the <m>(-2)</m>-eigenspace).</p>
      <image source="images/e3_pic1">
        <shortdescription><!-- FILL: concise image description --></shortdescription>
        <description><p><!-- FILL: complete image description --></p></description>
      </image>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        From the picture, we see <m>\lambda_1 = 1</m> has eigenvector
          <m>v_1 = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m>.  Also, <m>\lambda= -2</m> has eigenvector
          <m>v_2 = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m>.  Forming <m>P=\begin{pmatrix}v_1 \amp v_2\end{pmatrix}</m> and
          <m>D=\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -2\end{pmatrix}</m> we get <m>A=PDP^{-1}</m>, so
        <md>
          <mrow>A \amp  = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp -1</mrow>
          <mrow>2 \amp 1\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp -2\end{pmatrix} \dfrac{1}{3}
             \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>-2 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = \frac{1}{3} \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp -1</mrow>
          <mrow>2 \amp 1\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>4 \amp -2\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = \frac{1}{3} \begin{pmatrix}-3 \amp 3</mrow>
          <mrow>6 \amp 0\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>2 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Let <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 3 \\ 18 \amp 2\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}-2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 3\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp -5 \\ 18 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}^{-1}</m>.</p>
      <p>
        Find all values of <m>k</m> (if there are any) so that
        <md><mrow>A \begin{pmatrix}9 \\ k\end{pmatrix}= \begin{pmatrix}27 \\ 3k\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>We do not need to do any matrix multiplication, since <m>A</m> has been
        diagonalized for us:</p>
      <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p><m>\lambda = -2</m> is an eigenvalue with corresponding eigenspace
              Span<m>\left\{\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 18\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><m>\lambda = 3</m> is an eigenvalue with corresponding eigenspace
              Span<m>\left\{\begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m></p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      </p>
      <p>The problem is subtly asking us to find <m>k</m> so that <m>\begin{pmatrix}9 \\ k\end{pmatrix}</m> is
        in the <m>3</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m>.  This means <m>\begin{pmatrix}9 \\ k\end{pmatrix}</m> is a scalar
        multiple of <m>\begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m>, so <m>k = 6</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose <m>A</m> is a diagonalizable <m>n \times n</m> matrix, and
          suppose <m>B</m> is an <m>n \times n</m> matrix with the same eigenvalues of
          <m>A</m> and the same corresponding eigenspaces for each eigenvalue.  Is
          it necessarily true that <m>A=B</m>?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Yes.  Since <m>A</m> is diagonalizable, we can write
            <m>A = PDP^{-1}</m>, where the columns of <m>P</m> are a basis of
            <m>{\bf R}^n</m> consisting of eigenvectors of <m>A</m> and where <m>D</m> is the
            diagonal matrix with the corresponding eigenvalues.  Since <m>B</m> has
            the same eigenvalues of <m>A</m> and the same corresponding
            eigenspaces, we also have <m>B = PDP^{-1}</m>, so <m>A = B = PDP^{-1}</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Answer part (a) in the case that <m>A</m> is an <m>n \times n</m>
          matrix which is \textit{not} diagonalizable.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>No.  For example, consider <m>A= \begin{pmatrix}0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}</m> and
            <m>B = \begin{pmatrix}0 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}</m>.  Each matrix has only one eigenvalue
            <m>\lambda = 0</m> with algebraic multiplicity <m>2</m>, and the
            <m>0</m>-eigenspace for each matrix is the span of <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}</m>, yet
            <m>A \neq B</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the following matrices <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}3 \amp 1 \amp 4 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ -1 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}\sqrt{2} \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 70^{1/7} \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp \pi\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}3 \amp 1 \amp 4 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ -1 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}^{-1}</mrow></md>,
        <md><mrow>B = \begin{pmatrix}-3 \amp 3 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 3 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{1}{2}\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}\sqrt{2} \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 70^{1/7} \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp \pi\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}-3 \amp 3 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 3 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{1}{2}\end{pmatrix}^{-1}</mrow></md>.
        Without doing any computations, determine whether <m>A = B</m> or
        <m>A \neq B</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>We show that <m>A=B</m>.  By the Diagonalization Theorem, <m>A = PDP^{-1}</m>,
        where <m>D</m> is any diagonal matrix of eigenvalues of <m>A</m> (with their
        geometric multiplicities) and <m>P</m> is \textit{any} matrix whose
        column vectors form bases for the corresponding eigenspaces.</p>
      <p>The <m>\sqrt{2}</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m> is
        <m>\textup{Span}\left\{\begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 0 \\ -1\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>, which is the same as
        <m>\textup{Span}\left\{\begin{pmatrix}-3 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>.</p>
      <p>The <m>70^{1/7}</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m> is <m>\textup{Span}\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}</m> which
        is the same as <m>\textup{Span}\left\{\begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 3 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} \right\}</m>.</p>
      <p>The <m>\pi</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m> is
        <m>\textup{Span}\left\{\begin{pmatrix}4 \\ 2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>, which is the same as
        <m>\textup{Span}\left\{\begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 1 \\ \frac{1}{2}\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>.</p>
      <p>Therefore, by the Diagonalization Theorem, <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are two ways
        of writing the same matrix.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>More practice with diagonalization.</p>
      <p>Consider the matrix
      <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}-1& 5& 10 \\ 0& 4& 10 \\0 & 0 & 5 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Find the eigenvalues of <m>A</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>The matrix is upper-triangular, so its eigenvalues are
            its diagonal entries: <m>\lambda = -1</m>, <m>\lambda = 4</m>, and <m>\lambda = 5</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>For each eigenvalue of <m>A</m>, compute a basis for the
          corresponding eigenspace.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>The <m>(-1)</m>-eigenspace is <m>\Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>.</p>
        <p>The <m>4</m>-eigenspace is <m>\Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>.</p>
        <p>The <m>5</m>-eigenspace is <m>\Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}10 \\ 10 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Decide whether <m>A</m> is diagonalizable. If it is
          diagonalizable, find an invertible <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix <m>P</m> and a
          diagonal matrix <m>D</m> such that <m>A = PDP^{-1}</m>. If <m>A</m> is not
          diagonalizable, explain why.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>A</m> is diagonalizable: <m>A = PDP^{-1}</m> where the diagonal entries
              of <m>D</m> are the eigenvalues and the columns of <m>P</m> are eigenvectors written
              in the corresponding order.  Many answers are possible, for example:
          <md><mrow>P = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 1 \amp 10 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 10 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}, \qquad D = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 4 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 5\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <solution>
      <p>For this problem, we give the answers and leave the
        computations to the reader.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>More practice with diagonalization.</p>
      <p>Let <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</m>.  Is <m>A</m> diagonalizable?  If so, find a
      basis of <m>{\bf R}^3</m> consisting of eigenvectors of <m>A</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>For this problem, we give the answers and leave the computations to the
        reader.</p>
      <p>Solving <m>\det(A-\lambda I) = 0</m> gives <m>\lambda = 1</m> and <m>\lambda = 2</m>.</p>
      <p>The <m>1</m>-eigenspace is <m>\Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}0 \\ -1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>.</p>
      <p>The <m>2</m>-eigenspace is
        <m>\Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>.</p>
      <p>
        The set <m>\left\{\begin{pmatrix}0 \\ -1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} \right\}</m> is a basis
          for <m>{\bf R}^{3}</m> consisting of eigenvectors for <m>A</m>, so <m>A</m> is
          diagonalizable.  In fact, <m>A = PDP^{-1}</m> where the diagonal entries of <m>D</m> are
          the eigenvalues and the columns of <m>P</m> are eigenvectors written in the
          corresponding order.  Many answers are possible, for example:
        <md><mrow>P = \begin{pmatrix}0 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\ -1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}, \qquad D = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</exercises>\setcounter{problem}{0}

\newpage

\underline{5.5, Complex eigenvalues}

{\bf Important note}: In this section, as in the rest of Math 1553, the entries
of all matrices are real numbers unless specified otherwise.

<exercises>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="yes">
      <p>If <m>v</m> is an eigenvector for <m>A</m> and <m>v</m> has complex entries,
      then <m>i \cdot v</m> is also an eigenvector for <m>A</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        True: any scalar multiple of a complex eigenvector is also a complex
          eigenvector.  By linearity of matrix multiplication, we see that if
          <m>Av = \lambda v</m>, then
        <md><mrow>A(iv) = i Av= i(\lambda v) = \lambda(iv)</mrow></md>,
        so if <m>v</m> is an eigenvector then so is <m>iv</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="yes">
      <p>If <m>\begin{pmatrix}i \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m> is an eigenvector for <m>A</m>, then <m>\begin{pmatrix}i \\ -1\end{pmatrix}</m> is
      also an eigenvector for <m>A</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>True. Recall that the complex conjugate of any complex eigenvector is also a
        complex eigenvector, and any (nonzero) scalar multiple of a complex
        eigenvector is also a complex eigenvector.</p>
      <p>Since <m>v = \begin{pmatrix}i \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m> is an eigenvector, so is
        <m>\overline{v} = \begin{pmatrix}-i \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m>, and so is <m>-\overline{v} = \begin{pmatrix}i \\ -1\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>If <m>A</m> is a <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix with real entries, what are the possibilities
      for the number of different non-real eigenvalues of <m>A</m>?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>The maximum is <m>2</m>.  Every <m>3 \times 3</m> real matrix has at least one real
        eigenvalue, since its characteristic polynomial is a degree <m>3</m> polynomial
        with real coefficients and therefore must have at least one real root
        (straightforward application of end behavior of odd-degree polynomials and the
        Intermediate Value Theorem).</p>
      <p>It is possible for <m>A</m> to have exactly 1 real eigenvalue and <m>2</m> non-real
        eigenvalues.  For example, <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</m> has eigenvalues
        <m>1</m>, <m>i</m>, and <m>-i</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>If <m>A</m> is a <m>4 \times 4</m> matrix with real entries, what are the possibilities
      for the number of different non-real eigenvalues of <m>A</m>?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The max is <m>4</m>.  It is not easy to come up with such a matrix <m>A</m>, but it is
          possible.  For example,
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 2 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>
        has the eigenvalues <m>i</m>,
          <m>-i</m>, <m>2i</m>, and <m>-2i</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the matrix
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}3 \amp -7 \\ 1 \amp -1\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>
        Find all eigenvalues of <m>A</m>.  Simplify your answer.
         For the eigenvalue with negative imaginary part, find an eigenvector.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We compute the characteristic equation:
        <md><mrow>\begin{split}
                0= \det(A-\lambda I)=(3-\lambda)(-1-\lambda)+7=
                \lambda^2-2\lambda-3+7 = \lambda^2-2\lambda+4.
            \end{split}</mrow></md>
        By the quadratic formula,
        <md><mrow>\lambda = \frac{2\pm\sqrt{4-16}}2 = \dfrac{2 \pm 2i\sqrt{3}}{2}= 1 \pm
              \sqrt{3}i</mrow></md>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Let <m>\lambda = 1-\sqrt{3}i</m>.  Then
        <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rr|r}A-\lambda \amp I \amp 0\end{array}\right) = \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}2+\sqrt{3}i \amp -7 \amp 0 \\ \star \amp \star \amp 0\end{array}\right)
            \xrightarrow[R_1 = R_1/(2+\sqrt{3}i)]{\textup{destroy
                <m>R_2</m>}} \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp \frac{-7}{2+\sqrt{3}i} \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right) =\left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp -2+\sqrt{3}i \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)</mrow></md>
        So <m>x_1 = (2-\sqrt{3}i)x_2</m> and <m>x_2</m> is free. An eigenvector is
          <m>v= \begin{pmatrix}2-\sqrt{3}i \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>An alternative method for finding an eigenvector, using a trick you may have
        seen in class, is to take the first row <m>(a \ b)</m> of <m>A-\lambda I_2</m> to get an
        eigenvector <m>\begin{pmatrix}-b \\ a\end{pmatrix}</m>:</p>
      <p>
        <md><mrow>A - \lambda I_2 = \begin{pmatrix}2+\sqrt{3}i \amp -7 \\ \star \amp \star\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>
        Thus
          <m>\begin{pmatrix}7 \\ 2+\sqrt{3}i\end{pmatrix}</m> is an eigenvector for <m>\lambda</m>. This answer is
          equivalent to our answer <m>v</m> above since it is a nonzero scalar multiple of
          <m>v</m>, as <m>\begin{pmatrix}7 \\ 2+\sqrt{3}i\end{pmatrix} = (2+\sqrt{3}i)v</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the complex eigenvalues and a vector in each eigenspace for the following
         matrix.
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}3 \amp -2 \\ 4 \amp -1\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The characteristic equation is
        <md><mrow>0 = \det(A-\lambda I) = \lambda^2 - \Tr(A) I + \det(A)
          = \lambda^2 - 2 \lambda + 5</mrow></md>,
        so the eigenvalues are
        <md><mrow>\lambda = \dfrac{2 \pm \sqrt{(-2)^2-5(4)}}{2}
          = \dfrac{2 \pm \sqrt{-16}}{2} = \dfrac{2 \pm 4i}{2} = 1 \pm 2i</mrow></md>.
        We use the <m>2 \times 2</m> eigenvector trick: if <m>\lambda</m> is an eigenvalue of <m>A</m>
        and <m>(a \ b)</m> is the first row of <m>A-\lambda I_2</m>, then <m>\begin{pmatrix}-b \\ a\end{pmatrix}</m> is an
        eigenvector of <m>A</m> unless <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are both <m>0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>\lambda_1 = 1 + 2i</m>:
        <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{r|r}A-(1+2i)I \amp 0\end{array}\right) = \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}2-2i \amp -2 \amp 0 \\ (*) \amp (*) \amp 0\end{array}\right) = \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}a \amp b \amp 0 \\ (*) \amp (*) \amp 0\end{array}\right)</mrow></md>.
        Therefore, <m>v_1 = \begin{pmatrix}-b \\ a\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 2-2i\end{pmatrix}</m> is an eigenvector for
        <m>\lambda_1 = 1+2i</m>.
      </p>
      <p>We do not need to do any work to find an eigenvector for the other eigenvalue:
      <m>v_2 = \overline{v_1} = \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 2+2i\end{pmatrix}</m> is an eigenvector for
      <m>\lambda_2 = \overline{\lambda_1} = 1-2i</m>.</p>
      <p>There are many possible answers for <m>v_1</m> and <m>v_2</m>, and it is generally
      difficult to tell whether vectors are complex scalar multiples of each other.
      If you obtained different answers than this solution did for (say) <m>v_1</m>, you
      can check whether your answer is correct by computing <m>Av_1</m> and seeing if it is
      equal to <m>(1+2i)v_1</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the complex eigenvalues and eigenvectors for the following matrix.
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \amp -4 \\ 1 \amp -1\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find all eigenvalues and eigenvectors for the following matrix.
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0 \amp -2 \\ 1 \amp 3 \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Write a real <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix with eigenvalues <m>5 \pm 2i</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
</exercises>\setcounter{problem}{0}

\newpage

\underline{5.6, Stochastic Matrices}

<exercises>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Is there a stochastic matrix where the <m>1</m>-eigenspace has dimension greater than <m>1</m>?</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the steady-state vector for the matrix
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}1/2 \amp 1/3 \\ 1/2 \amp 2/3\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        What vector does <m>A^n \begin{pmatrix}5 \\ 7\end{pmatrix}</m> approach as <m>n \to \infty</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the steady-state vector for the matrix
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}1/3 \amp 1/5 \amp 1/4 \\ 1/3 \amp 2/5 \amp 1/2 \\ 1/3 \amp 2/5 \amp 1/4\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        What vector does <m>A^n \begin{pmatrix}5 \\ 7 \\ 38\end{pmatrix}</m> approach as <m>n \to \infty</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Consider an internet with four pages: 1, 2, 3, and 4.</p>
      <p>Page 1 links to pages 2 and 3.</p>
      <p>Page 2 links to pages 3 and 4.</p>
      <p>Page 3 links to pages 1 and 4.</p>
      <p>Page 4 links to page 1.</p>
      <p>(i) Write the importance matrix <m>A</m> for this internet.</p>
      <p>
        (ii) <m>A</m> has a unique steady-state vector <m>w</m>.  Find <m>w</m>.  You may use the fact
        that
        <md><mrow>A \begin{pmatrix}8 \\ 4 \\ 6 \\ 5\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}8 \\ 4 \\ 6 \\ 5\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        (i) The importance matrix is
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}0 \amp 0 \amp 1/2 \amp 1 \\ 1/2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 1/2 \amp 1/2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1/2 \amp 1/2 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
      <p>(ii) <m>w=\dfrac{1}{8+4+6+5} \begin{pmatrix}8 \\ 4 \\ 6 \\ 5\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}8/23 \\ 4/23 \\ 6/23 \\ 5/23\end{pmatrix}.</m></p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Courage Soda and Dexter Soda compete for a market of <m>210</m> customers
      who drink soda each day.</p>
      <p>Today, Courage has <m>80</m> customers and Dexter has <m>130</m> customers.
      Each day:</p>
      <p><m>70\%</m> of Courage Soda's customers keep drinking
      	Courage Soda, while <m>30\%</m> switch to Dexter Soda.</p>
      <p><m>40\%</m> of Dexter Soda's customers keep drinking Dexter Soda, while
      	<m>60\%</m> switch to Courage Soda.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Write a stochastic matrix <m>A</m> and a vector <m>x</m> so
              that <m>Ax</m> will give the number of customers for Courage Soda and
              Dexter Soda (in that order) tomorrow.</p>
        <p>\ifanswers</p>
        <p><m>A= \begin{pmatrix}0.7 \amp 0.6 \\ 0.3 \amp 0.4\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>x=\begin{pmatrix}80 \\ 130\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
        <p>\else
        	\fi</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Find the steady-state vector for <m>A</m>.
        	\ifanswers</p>
        <p><m>\left(\begin{array}{r|r}A-I \amp 0\end{array}\right)= \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}-0.3 \amp 0.6 \amp 0 \\ 0.3 \amp -0.6 \amp 0\end{array}\right)
            \xrightarrow[R_1=R_1/(-0.3)]{R_2=R_2+R_1} \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)</m></p>
        <p>so <m>x_1 = 2x_2</m> and <m>x_2</m> is free.  A <m>1</m>-eigenvector is
            <m>\begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m>, so the steady state vector is
            <m>w= \dfrac{1}{2+1} \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}2/3 \\ 1/3\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
        <p>\else
        	\fi</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Use your answer from (b) to determine the following:
            in the long run, roughly how many daily customers will Courage
            Soda have?
            \ifanswers</p>
        <p>As <m>n</m> gets large, <m>A^n \begin{pmatrix}80 \\ 130\end{pmatrix}</m>
              approaches <m>210 \begin{pmatrix}2/3 \\ 1/3\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}140 \\ 70\end{pmatrix}</m>.  Courage will
              have roughly <m>140</m> customers.  \else \vspace{3cm} \fi</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
</exercises>\setcounter{problem}{0}


